# TODO - Documentation authority taxonomy

## Objective

Define the exact documentation ownership model before the planning corpus is
promoted into durable records. `docs/technical/`, `docs/research/`,
`docs/legal/`, and `docs/bibliography/` are the four documentation authority
families, and each family owns its own `adr/` directory for decisions local to
that family. A global `docs/adr/` is forbidden so unrelated decisions cannot
collapse into an STM-style ADR monolith. ADRs contain bounded decisions and
tradeoffs; technical specifications, research findings, legal analysis, and
bibliography records remain ordinary documentation under their owning family.
`docs/cspell/` remains the editorial support surface used by CSpell and is not a
fifth authority family. Jig will eventually validate this topology; Malbolge
must not grow a parallel documentation linter to enforce it.

## Acceptance

- Exactly four documentation authority families exist: technical, research,
  legal, and bibliography; each owns a local `adr/` directory.
- No global `docs/adr/` exists, and ADRs are bounded decisions rather than a
  catch-all location for specifications, findings, legal analysis, source
  records, or chat history.
- `docs/cspell/` remains inside `docs/` as editorial tooling support and is
  explicitly not treated as a fifth authority family.
- The family ownership rules distinguish decisions, current technical behavior,
  research evidence, legal analysis, and external-source evidence without
  duplicate authority.
- The design is intended for later Jig validation; this repository does not
  introduce a parallel documentation-linter implementation.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `docs/` taxonomy plus the future
  family-local ADR governance records.
- Evidence must compare the selected model against the observed
  strengths/weaknesses of STM-style global ADR organization and SHAR/Jig
  family-separated documentation without copying their unrelated product
  taxonomy.
